---
title: Options
order: 1
---
# Options

`mdsvex()` takes one object of options. They apply to every document.

```ts
interface MdsvexOptions {
	extensions?: string[];
	frontmatter?: {
		parse?: (raw: string) => Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined;
	};
	templates?: Record<
		string,
		string | URL | { component: string | URL; components?: string | URL }
	>;
	select_template?: (
		id: string,
		metadata: Record<string, unknown>
	) => string | false | undefined;
	components?: string | URL | (string | URL)[];
	component_mode?: 'markdown' | 'all';
	plugins?: Bundle[];
	parse_plugins?: ParsePlugin[];
	highlight?: PluginHighlightOptions | Highlighter | false;
	math?: MathOptions | MathRenderer | false;
	footnotes?: { label?: string; back_label?: string; strict?: boolean };
	unwrap_images?: boolean;
	import_images?: boolean | { enhanced?: boolean };
	wrap_static?: boolean;
	links?: {
		routes?: string;
		base?: true | string | { import: string; from: string };
	};
	rewrite?: (
		url: string,
		ctx: { filename: string | undefined; type: 'link' | 'image'; written: string }
	) => string | null | undefined | void;
	heading_ids?:
		| boolean
		| { slug?: (text: string, heading: { depth: number }) => string };
	smart_punctuation?:
		| boolean
		| {
				quotes?: boolean | [string, string, string, string];
				dashes?: boolean;
				ellipses?: boolean;
		  };
	text?: { code_blocks?: boolean };
}
```

::marks

## `extensions`

::::plate[The file extensions mdsvex compiles](mark=".svx", tone=other)
The file extensions mdsvex compiles. To use `.md` files as components too:

```ts
extensions: string[] = ['.svx'];
```

```js
mdsvex({ extensions: ['.svx', '.md'] });
```

:::notes
SvelteKit picks them up from here, see [SvelteKit](/docs/learn/quick-start#sveltekit).
:::
::::

## `frontmatter`

::::plate[Replaces the YAML parser](mark="---", tone=text)
Replaces the built-in YAML parser.

```ts
frontmatter: { parse?: (raw: string) => Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined };
```

:::notes
See [custom frontmatter parsers](/docs/guides/frontmatter#custom-frontmatter-parsers). On the atlas: [frontmatter](/atlas#frontmatter).
:::
::::

## `templates`

::::plate[Components that wrap documents](mark="#lib", tone=comp)
Components that wrap documents, by name. The `default` entry wraps every document that doesn't pick another.

```ts
templates: Record<string, string | URL | { component: string | URL; components?: string | URL }>;
```

:::notes
See [Templates](/docs/guides/templates). On the atlas: [template](/atlas#template).
:::
::::

## `select_template`

::::plate[Picks a document's template](mark="=>", tone=expr)
Picks the template of a document whose frontmatter doesn't.

```ts
select_template: (id: string, metadata: Record<string, unknown>) => string | false | undefined;
```

:::notes
See [choosing a template](/docs/guides/templates#choosing-a-template). `id` is the path of the file. [`compile`](/docs/reference/compile)'s own `select_template` gets only the metadata, see [`select_template` with `compile`](/docs/guides/templates#choosing-a-template).
:::
::::

## `components`

::::plate[Replacements in every document](mark="<p>", tone=comp)
One or more modules whose exports replace elements in every document, below any template's own replacements.

```ts
components: string | URL | (string | URL)[];
```

:::notes
See [Custom components](/docs/guides/components).
:::
::::

## `component_mode`

::::plate[Which elements can be replaced](mark="'all'", tone=comp)
Which elements can be replaced. `'markdown'` only replaces elements written as markdown, `'all'` also replaces HTML you type.

```ts
component_mode: 'markdown' | 'all' = 'markdown';
```

:::notes
See [`component_mode: 'all'`](/docs/guides/components#component_mode-all).
:::
::::

## `highlight`

::::plate[How code is highlighted](mark="```", tone=fence)
How code is highlighted. Options configure twinkleplop, a function replaces it, and `false` renders plain code.

```ts
highlight: PluginHighlightOptions | Highlighter | false = {};
```

:::notes
See [Syntax highlighting](/docs/reference/highlighting). On the atlas: [code fence](/atlas#code-fence).
:::
::::

## `math`

::::plate[How math renders](mark="$", tone=code)
How a `math` fence and inline math render. `renderer` picks `'temml'`, the default, or `'katex'`, and `options` goes to that library. A function is a renderer of your own, and `false` leaves the fence a code block and inline math inline code.

```ts
math: MathOptions | MathRenderer | false = {};
```

:::notes
See [Math](/docs/reference/math). On the atlas: [math](/atlas#math).
:::
::::

## `footnotes`

::::plate[The names of the footnotes section](mark="[^]", tone=link)
The accessible names of the [footnotes](/docs/reference/footnotes) section and of its back links, for a site that is not in English.

```ts
footnotes: { label?: string; back_label?: string; strict?: boolean } = {};
```

:::notes
`strict: false` builds a document whose footnotes a compile rejects, with warnings in place of the error. On the atlas: [footnote](/atlas#footnote).
:::
::::

## `unwrap_images`

::::plate[An image on a line of its own](mark="![]", tone=link)
An image on a line of its own is a paragraph, so it renders as `<p><img /></p>`. That is a problem once an `img` [replacement](/docs/guides/components) renders a block element, because a `<figure>` is not allowed inside a `<p>`:

```ts
unwrap_images: boolean = false;
```

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/Figure.svelte -->
<script>
	let { src, alt, title } = $props();
</script>

<figure>
	<img {src} {alt} />
	{#if title}<figcaption>{title}</figcaption>{/if}
</figure>
```

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as img } from './Figure.svelte';
```

```js config
mdsvex({ components: '#lib/markdown.js', unwrap_images: true });
```

:::notes
On the atlas: [image](/atlas#image).
:::
::::

::::plate[A paragraph of only images](mark="<p>", tone=para)
With `unwrap_images`, a paragraph that holds only images gets no `<p>`, so the figure stands on its own:

```svx result config
Sales grew all year.

![A chart](/chart.png "Sales by month")
```

:::notes
Several images in one paragraph are all unwrapped, on one line or on lines of their own. So is an image inside a link, such as `[![A chart](/chart.png)](/full.png)`, where the link stays around the image. It works the same inside block quotes, list items, directives and HTML elements.
:::

:::edges
- A paragraph with any text beside the image keeps its `<p>`, and so does one with a hard line break between two images.
:::
::::

`compile()` takes the option too, and the editor reads it from the manifest the plugin writes, or from `unwrap_images` in an `mdsvex.config.json`.

## `wrap_static`

::::plate[Static blocks as one node](mark="<div>", tone=comp)
Wraps each run of two or more top level blocks with no Svelte in them in a `<div style="display: contents">`, so hydration steps over the run as one node instead of one node for each block:

```ts
wrap_static: boolean = false;
```

```js config
mdsvex({ wrap_static: true });
```

```svx result config
# Title

Some text.

<Counter />

More {count}.
```

:::notes
`compile()` takes the option too.
:::

:::edges
- The page looks the same, but a child selector such as `article > p` no longer matches a block inside a wrapper, and a sibling selector such as `h1 + p` doesn't match across the edge of one.
:::
::::

## `heading_ids`

::::plate[An id from the heading's text](mark="##", tone=heading)
Gives every markdown heading an `id` made from its text, so a link can point at it:

```ts
heading_ids: boolean | { slug?: (text: string, heading: { depth: number }) => string } = false;
```

```js config
mdsvex({ heading_ids: true });
```

```svx result config
## Getting _started_

## Getting started

## The `highlight` option
```

:::notes
The id is the one GitHub makes: the text in lowercase, punctuation dropped, each space a hyphen, and letters of any script kept. A second heading with the same text gets `-1`, a third `-2`.

The text is what a reader sees. Emphasis, links and code contribute their text without the markup, and an image contributes its alt text. What Svelte renders contributes nothing, because it isn't known until the page runs: an expression such as `{version}`, a component and a directive.

On the atlas: [heading](/atlas#heading).
:::

:::edges
- A heading that is left with no text, such as `## {title}`, gets no `id`.
:::
::::

::::plate[An id that is already there](mark="id=", tone=heading)
A heading that already has an `id` keeps it, whether a [parse plugin](/docs/plugins/writing/parse-plugins#parse_plugins) set it or you typed the heading as HTML, and no id made after it repeats it. Headings typed as HTML are otherwise left alone.

:::notes
Each heading gets its id as it closes, from the ids before it, and an id never changes once given. Footnotes are numbered at the end, so a footnote whose id a heading took moves aside instead: `## Fn 1` keeps `fn-1` and the footnote `[^1]` becomes `fn-1-1`.
:::

:::edges
- An id you write yourself, on a heading typed as HTML or through a plugin that reads one from your text, is kept, and one that an element before it already has fails the compile with a `HeadingIdError` that names both:

```svx error config
## Intro

<h2 id="intro">Again</h2>
```
:::
::::

::::plate[A `slug` of your own](mark="slug", tone=expr)
For another scheme, pass a `slug` function. It gets the text and the depth of the heading, and mdsvex numbers duplicates in what it returns. An empty string leaves the heading without an `id`. `heading_slug` is the default:

```js
import { mdsvex, heading_slug } from 'mdsvex';

mdsvex({
	heading_ids: {
		slug: (text, { depth }) => (depth === 1 ? 'top' : heading_slug(text))
	}
});
```
::::

::::plate[An anchor from a plugin](mark="#", tone=link)
The id is there for the function a parse plugin returns from its handler, which runs when the heading closes, and for every sequential plugin, so one can add an anchor that points at its heading:

```js config
const anchors = {
	heading: {
		parse(node) {
			return () => {
				node.prepend('link', { href: `#${node.attrs.id}`, 'aria-hidden': 'true' });
			};
		}
	}
};

mdsvex({ heading_ids: true, parse_plugins: [anchors] });
```

```svx result config
## Getting started
```

:::notes
[`@mdsvex/plugin-anchors`](/docs/plugins/anchors) is that plugin, with a choice of where the link goes.
:::
::::

A [replacement](/docs/guides/components) for a heading gets the `id` as a prop. `compile()` takes the option too. A streaming render makes the same ids as the heading arrives: `make_heading_ids()` from `mdsvex/compile`, given a `slug` or not, goes to the builder with `set_headings`. The editor reads the option from the manifest the plugin writes, or from `heading_ids: true` in an `mdsvex.config.json`, which can't hold a `slug` function.

## `smart_punctuation`

::::plate[The punctuation a typesetter would use](mark="--", tone=text)
Turns the punctuation you can type into the punctuation a typesetter would use:

```ts
smart_punctuation:
	| boolean
	| { quotes?: boolean | [string, string, string, string]; dashes?: boolean; ellipses?: boolean }
	= false;
```

```js config
mdsvex({ smart_punctuation: true });
```

```svx result config
"quoted", 'quoted', it's, 1990--2020, wait --- what, and so on...
```

:::notes
It is off by default, so a document renders the characters it has unless you ask. mdsvex 0.x had it on, see [smart punctuation in 0.x](/docs/guides/migrating-from-0x#smart-punctuation-in-0x).
:::
::::

::::added
:::add[`"quoted"`](mark='"', tone=text, id=smart-double)
curly double quotes
:::

:::add[`'quoted'`, `it's`](mark="'", tone=text, id=smart-single)
curly single quotes, and the apostrophe
:::

:::add[`1990--2020`](mark="--", tone=text, id=smart-en)
two hyphens are an en dash
:::

:::add[`wait --- what`](mark="---", tone=text, id=smart-em)
three hyphens are an em dash
:::

:::add[`and so on...`](mark="...", tone=text, id=smart-ellipsis)
three dots are an ellipsis
:::
::::

::::plate[Only those five](mark="----", tone=text)
Only those five are replaced. A run of any other length stays as written, so four hyphens or four dots are still four:

```svx result config
Four ---- hyphens and four .... dots. Run it with --watch, but a -- b, a--b and 1--2.
```

:::edges
- Two hyphens after a space and before a letter are a command line flag and stay too, so `run it with --watch` is safe, while `a -- b`, `a--b` and `1--2` get a dash.
:::
::::

::::plate[Which way a quote faces](mark="'", tone=text)
A quote opens after a space, at the start of a paragraph, heading, list item or table cell, and after an opening bracket, a dash or another quote that just opened. Anywhere else it closes, which also makes an apostrophe:

```svx result config
"_word_" and _"word"_, the `code`'s, the '90s and 6'2".
```

:::notes
A quote with a space on both sides is left straight. Markup is looked through: the quotes in `_"word"_` and `"_word_"` face the same way, and the `'s` after a code span, a link or `{name}` is an apostrophe.
:::

:::edges
- Quotes are not paired, so two cases come out wrong: an apostrophe at the start of a word opens (`'90s`, `rock 'n' roll`), and feet and inches close (`6'2"`). Type the character you want there, `’90s` or `6′2″`. A character you type yourself is never changed.
:::
::::

::::plate[Quotes of another language](mark="«", tone=text)
Each kind can be turned off, and `quotes` takes the four strings of another language, the opening and closing double quote and then the opening and closing single quote:

```js config
mdsvex({
	smart_punctuation: {
		dashes: false,
		// French, with a no-break space inside the guillemets
		quotes: ['«\u00a0', '\u00a0»', '“', '”']
	}
});
```

```svx result config
"Bonjour", 'salut', l'ami -- 1990--2020.
```

:::notes
German is `['„', '“', '‚', '‘']`. An apostrophe between two letters or digits is always `’`, whatever the closing single quote is. There is one style for the whole site, mdsvex does not read a document's language.
:::
::::

::::plate[Only prose is touched](mark="`", tone=code)
Only prose is touched. These stay exactly as written:

- code spans, fenced code, highlighted code and math
- Svelte expressions and blocks, such as `{"a--b"}` and `{#if x === "y"}`
- attributes of elements and components, such as `<Box --gap="1--2">`
- urls, and the text of an autolink such as `<https://example.com/a--b>`
- the `alt` text of an image and the `title` of a link or an image
- text inside `<code>`, `<kbd>`, `<pre>`, `<samp>`, `<var>`, `<tt>` and `<textarea>`, and the contents of `<script>` and `<style>`
- character references, so `&quot;` and `&#39;` are a way to keep a straight quote
- a character behind a backslash, so `\"`, `\'`, `\--` and `\...` stay literal
- frontmatter, HTML comments and thematic breaks

:::notes
Text inside a component or any other element is prose and is changed.
:::
::::

[`heading_ids`](/docs/reference/options#heading_ids) makes its ids from the text as written, so turning this on does not change any anchor. `compile()` takes the option too. It belongs to the compile and to the incremental HTML renderer, so the streaming Svelte renderer shows text as written. The editor reads the option from the manifest the plugin writes, or from `smart_punctuation` in an `mdsvex.config.json`.

## `text`

::::plate[What a `?text` import reads](mark="?text", tone=text)
What a [`?text`](/docs/guides/search-and-feeds) import reads. `code_blocks: false` leaves out code fences, `math` fences and `<pre>` elements, so a search index holds only prose:

```ts
text: { code_blocks?: boolean } = {};
```

```js
mdsvex({ text: { code_blocks: false } });
```

:::notes
[`compile_text`](/docs/guides/search-and-feeds#text-with-compile) takes `code_blocks` beside the options of `compile`.
:::
::::
